WEBVTT


00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.300
the most exciting thing in software development to me right now

00:00:03.301 --> 00:00:06.901
is software factories literally software that self improves

00:00:06.902 --> 00:00:09.061
that as you collect feedback and telemetry

00:00:09.062 --> 00:00:10.921
the software gets better automatically

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:13.020
I've been using this approach for the last few months

00:00:13.021 --> 00:00:15.621
to push the boundaries of how much we can automate

00:00:15.622 --> 00:00:18.521
setting bugs and improvements directly through agentic loops

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.660
and having better software come out the other side

00:00:20.661 --> 00:00:22.861
while making sure human touch points can always guard

00:00:22.862 --> 00:00:25.381
and take over as much as needed and I've open sourced a set of

00:00:25.382 --> 00:00:28.461
skills that you can use or customize to to run your own software

00:00:28.462 --> 00:00:31.301
factory out of Codex or Cloud Code or your choice of tools

00:00:31.302 --> 00:00:33.141
and it's all customizable configurable

00:00:33.142 --> 00:00:34.621
let me walk you through how it works

00:00:34.622 --> 00:00:36.421
the short of it is this pretty basic

00:00:36.422 --> 00:00:38.821
loop we want any feedback or telemetry

00:00:38.822 --> 00:00:41.141
such as century errors error logs

00:00:41.142 --> 00:00:44.581
performance and uptime metrics of those that are verifiably fixable

00:00:44.582 --> 00:00:47.561
as in an agent can reproduce the issue make a fix

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.180
verify the fix is correct and send a pull request

00:00:50.181 --> 00:00:53.261
we want to just automatically delegate those two agents those agents

00:00:53.262 --> 00:00:55.261
can be right there on your local computer using

00:00:55.262 --> 00:00:57.301
Codex or Cloud Code or whatever tool you prefer

00:00:57.302 --> 00:00:58.581
and when the fix is verified

00:00:58.582 --> 00:00:59.801
they're going to a pull request

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.740
it's decided whether it needs a human review

00:01:01.741 --> 00:01:03.421
or it's okay for a pure agent review

00:01:03.422 --> 00:01:06.301
when CI agent review and potential human review passes

00:01:06.302 --> 00:01:08.861
the software gets shipped and the loop starts again now obviously

00:01:08.862 --> 00:01:09.341
there's some pretty

00:01:09.342 --> 00:01:11.381
important things you need to do to make sure that you don't actually

00:01:11.382 --> 00:01:13.681
break things or make your code quality worse

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.820
that's why I'm going to walk you through the actual workflow that I use with the actual

00:01:16.821 --> 00:01:18.061
skills every day

00:01:18.062 --> 00:01:19.861
the vast majority of the work on my open

00:01:19.862 --> 00:01:23.141
source agent native project is built by autonomous software factories

00:01:23.142 --> 00:01:26.001
incorporating Github issues in app feedback

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:27.740
internal feedback and other sources

00:01:27.741 --> 00:01:29.861
and verifying and pushing fixes to the framework

00:01:29.862 --> 00:01:32.461
as well as a bunch of hosted agent native apps

00:01:32.462 --> 00:01:33.861
that we build and maintain

00:01:33.862 --> 00:01:36.981
for things like a generic design generation slides analytics

00:01:36.982 --> 00:01:39.181
loom alternatives mail calendar whatever

00:01:39.182 --> 00:01:40.381
these apps are all free and open

00:01:40.382 --> 00:01:43.061
source so you can look at the code yourself and they're non trivial

00:01:43.062 --> 00:01:45.401
they do things like full on Figma style editing

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.180
and generation of designs slide editing like Google Slides

00:01:48.181 --> 00:01:50.741
calendar management you name it they're very modern

00:01:50.742 --> 00:01:51.741
they're fully agentic

00:01:51.742 --> 00:01:54.261
every single thing that you I can do the Asian can do

00:01:54.262 --> 00:01:57.141
you can use them as pure visual agents purify eyes

00:01:57.142 --> 00:01:58.581
but the best is mixing a bit of both

00:01:58.582 --> 00:02:01.021
so these aren't small apps they're built in pretty novel ways

00:02:01.022 --> 00:02:02.621
yet the majority of work I do

00:02:02.622 --> 00:02:04.021
is designing and optimizing

00:02:04.022 --> 00:02:06.181
and assessing the systems for building the software

00:02:06.182 --> 00:02:07.761
as opposed to writing the code myself

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.260
that's what my team focuses on to so let's start at the beginning

00:02:10.261 --> 00:02:11.941
and show you how this works everything

00:02:11.942 --> 00:02:13.741
ultimately starts with input sources

00:02:13.742 --> 00:02:15.401
if you're doing things the traditional way

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:18.300
product team and engineering teams will look at things like feedback

00:02:18.301 --> 00:02:20.981
submitted potentially via Slack or in your product

00:02:20.982 --> 00:02:24.121
or through customer interviews also you look at issues and telemetry

00:02:24.122 --> 00:02:25.581
is uptime reduced has P90

00:02:25.582 --> 00:02:28.381
response time increased is there a spike in new errors and century

00:02:28.382 --> 00:02:30.541
or for an open source project like this are there Git

00:02:30.542 --> 00:02:33.521
issues and PRs being filed all of those become the inputs

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:34.860
that we start making decisions on

00:02:34.861 --> 00:02:36.341
that's where our first skill comes into play

00:02:36.342 --> 00:02:37.661
it's called factory collect

00:02:37.662 --> 00:02:39.461
first thing it does is it collects

00:02:39.462 --> 00:02:41.341
and looks at these input sources on a loop

00:02:41.342 --> 00:02:44.001
that's where I have a ton of these things running on scheduled tasks

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.300
I use Kodak for this the same thing works in Cloud Code

00:02:46.301 --> 00:02:47.661
I have a slight preference for Kodak

00:02:47.662 --> 00:02:49.461
which I'll get back to but it really doesn't matter

00:02:49.462 --> 00:02:50.981
whatever coding agent you use

00:02:50.982 --> 00:02:52.861
should have a way to schedule tasks that could run

00:02:52.862 --> 00:02:54.341
hourly daily whatever

00:02:54.342 --> 00:02:57.321
and the factory collect one just runs every several hours

00:02:57.322 --> 00:02:59.041
it'll collect from all those inputs

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:00.300
and then run through a policy

00:03:00.301 --> 00:03:02.181
all of this is configured in a YAML file

00:03:02.182 --> 00:03:04.461
so we can list out our sources and create our own

00:03:04.462 --> 00:03:05.621
and then put them into workflows

00:03:05.622 --> 00:03:07.901
I'll mention in a bit what some of these things mean but it's

00:03:07.902 --> 00:03:09.101
important to know that you can add prompts

00:03:09.102 --> 00:03:11.541
to any of these things too so configure them precisely

00:03:11.542 --> 00:03:14.021
or just apply prompts in natural language or both

00:03:14.022 --> 00:03:16.561
by default my factories have a simple fixed policy

00:03:16.562 --> 00:03:18.781
if it's a clear issue that is verifiable

00:03:18.782 --> 00:03:21.341
aka an agent can reproduce the issue itself

00:03:21.342 --> 00:03:24.821
fix it and then verify the fix and send a PR the policy allows

00:03:24.822 --> 00:03:27.781
which means in Codex it spawns its own isolated work tree

00:03:27.782 --> 00:03:30.941
does the verification fix and opens or updates a PR

00:03:30.942 --> 00:03:33.141
the reason I have a slight preference for Codex for this

00:03:33.142 --> 00:03:36.121
is because the Luna model is so insanely cheap

00:03:36.122 --> 00:03:37.061
you can run your factory at

00:03:37.062 --> 00:03:40.661
very high volumes paying almost nothing I create literally dozens of

00:03:40.662 --> 00:03:44.781
PRs that are merged per day all powered by Luna on max settings

00:03:44.782 --> 00:03:48.261
and I don't even hit the limits of my 200 dollar ChatGPT Pro account

00:03:48.262 --> 00:03:50.941
that's awesome this works in cloud too but you're gonna hit limits

00:03:50.942 --> 00:03:54.261
and if your factory is doing big scale cost does start to matter

00:03:54.262 --> 00:03:57.021
especially when you factor in that verification is so key

00:03:57.022 --> 00:03:59.281
many things need to be verified entered in a browser

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:01.820
that consumes tokens automating screenshotting

00:04:01.821 --> 00:04:03.761
and reproducing and verifying every time

00:04:03.762 --> 00:04:06.261
but as you might have assumed the verification part is essential

00:04:06.262 --> 00:04:08.621
you don't want agents just guessing and throwing up PR

00:04:08.622 --> 00:04:11.781
for whatever they think might fix it we need to know it's reproduced

00:04:11.782 --> 00:04:14.341
it's fixed before we get any autonomous

00:04:14.342 --> 00:04:17.141
factory PR now if anything doesn't match the policy

00:04:17.142 --> 00:04:19.941
it goes all the way over here and it goes to humans

00:04:19.942 --> 00:04:21.581
human inputs are batch separately

00:04:21.582 --> 00:04:23.481
that's a separate automation I'll get back to that

00:04:23.482 --> 00:04:25.101
when the agent does create a PR

00:04:25.102 --> 00:04:27.101
then another skill in automation kicks in

00:04:27.102 --> 00:04:29.981
I have an every few hours automation that reviews PRs now it's

00:04:29.982 --> 00:04:30.941
important to note there's multiple

00:04:30.942 --> 00:04:33.461
layers of review here if we go to the agent native pull request

00:04:33.462 --> 00:04:34.741
and open up a PR

00:04:34.742 --> 00:04:38.321
we will see that your standard review bot comments will be pushed

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:39.020
we have our own agent

00:04:39.021 --> 00:04:42.341
for this but you can actually run that off of a code or codex using a skill

00:04:42.342 --> 00:04:45.101
and a loop as well and the agent that sends the PR

00:04:45.102 --> 00:04:48.061
uses the factory babysit PR skill to monitor the PR

00:04:48.062 --> 00:04:51.161
they are required to verify and address every single comment

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.980
or CI failure if they verify and agree with the comment

00:04:53.981 --> 00:04:56.061
aka it's in line with the initial intent

00:04:56.062 --> 00:04:58.281
and it is a true issue they will comment back

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:00.740
in this case if it's running from Codex commenting as me

00:05:00.741 --> 00:05:02.181
or if they disagree they just reply

00:05:02.182 --> 00:05:03.661
with why they are not going to do it

00:05:03.662 --> 00:05:07.121
then the separate review PRS agents looks over the open PRS

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:10.280
ensures everything's been addressed ensure the CI passes

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:13.120
there's no merge conflicts and then invokes an approval policy

00:05:13.121 --> 00:05:16.261
approval policies are customizable by you with configs and prompts

00:05:16.262 --> 00:05:19.341
and basically decides are we okay with this auto merging and deploying

00:05:19.342 --> 00:05:21.701
because it had thorough tests it was clearly verified

00:05:21.702 --> 00:05:24.461
and it was deemed relatively safe it was a C s s fix

00:05:24.462 --> 00:05:25.981
it was a highly unittestable fix

00:05:25.982 --> 00:05:28.941
whatever or does it need human review if it needs human review

00:05:28.942 --> 00:05:30.181
it waits on the human review

00:05:30.182 --> 00:05:32.661
and then merge if allowed it gets shipped end to end

00:05:32.662 --> 00:05:37.441
and that's the super basic workflow collect babysit review ship

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:39.980
repeat at any point when a policy doesn't match

00:05:39.981 --> 00:05:41.261
set it aside for humans

00:05:41.262 --> 00:05:43.621
either tagging them or aggregating for the humans

